      <article class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed space-y-6">
        <p>In a car accident claim, fault is everything. It determines who pays, how much they pay, and whether you can collect anything at all. Yet most accident victims do not understand how fault is actually determined — or how even a small assigned fault percentage can dramatically reduce their settlement.</p>
        <p>This guide explains exactly how fault works: how insurers decide it, how state law governs it, and what you can do when the other side is trying to pin blame on you unfairly.</p>

        <h2 id="what-is-fault" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">What Does "Fault" Mean in a Car Accident?</h2>
        <p>In legal terms, fault in a car accident refers to which driver's <strong>negligence</strong> caused the collision. Negligence means a failure to exercise the level of care that a reasonable person would exercise in the same situation. Every driver has a legal duty of care to operate their vehicle safely. When a driver breaches that duty — by speeding, running a red light, texting, following too closely, or failing to yield — and that breach causes an accident and resulting injury, they are "at fault" and legally liable for the damages.</p>
        <p>Fault can be:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-4 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>100% one driver:</strong> Most rear-end collisions, red light violations, and wrong-way driving cases</li>
          <li><strong>Shared between drivers:</strong> Both drivers contributed to the accident through different acts of negligence</li>
          <li><strong>Disputed:</strong> Both sides claim the other was at fault, and the evidence is unclear</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="how-determined" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">How Insurance Companies Determine Fault</h2>
        <p>Insurance adjusters — not police officers, not courts — make the initial fault determination in most car accident claims. Here is what they look at:</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Police Report</h3>
        <p>The police accident report is often the single most important document in a fault determination. Officers document their observations: skid marks, vehicle positions, damage patterns, witness statements, and any traffic violations cited. If a driver received a citation — for running a stop sign, DUI, speeding — that citation is powerful evidence of fault. Note that officers only write narratives and citations; they do not assign legal fault percentages, which is determined by insurance companies and ultimately courts.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Physical Evidence</h3>
        <p>The point of impact on the vehicles tells a story. A rear-end collision with damage to the front of Car B and the rear of Car A shows B hit A from behind. A T-bone collision's damage pattern reveals which car entered the intersection first. Photos taken at the scene — skid marks, resting positions, traffic control devices — all inform the adjuster's fault assessment.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Driver and Witness Statements</h3>
        <p>Both drivers give their accounts of the accident. Uninvolved witnesses who have no stake in the outcome carry the most credibility. This is why collecting witness contact information at the scene is so valuable — independent third-party accounts often resolve disputed fault cases.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Traffic Laws</h3>
        <p>Adjusters apply state traffic laws to the facts. If Driver A failed to yield while turning left and Driver B was going straight, state traffic law establishes that A had a duty to yield — making A presumptively at fault. Each state's vehicle code defines right-of-way rules, speed limits, following distances, and other standards that govern fault determinations.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Accident Reconstruction</h3>
        <p>For serious accidents with disputed liability, professional accident reconstructionists — engineers who specialize in analyzing crash physics — may be retained to produce expert opinions on vehicle speeds, points of impact, and causation. Their findings carry substantial weight with insurers and juries.</p>

        <h2 id="negligence-types" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Comparative vs. Contributory Negligence — The Biggest Legal Difference in Personal Injury Law</h2>
        <p>How shared fault affects your recovery depends entirely on which negligence system your state uses. This is one of the most consequential aspects of car accident law, and it varies significantly by state.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Pure Comparative Negligence</h3>
        <p>In pure comparative negligence states, you can recover damages even if you are mostly at fault — your recovery is simply reduced by your percentage of fault. If you are 70% at fault for an accident and your damages are $100,000, you can still recover $30,000.</p>
        <p><strong>Pure comparative negligence states:</strong> California, New York, Florida, Alaska, Arizona, Kentucky, Louisiana, Mississippi, Missouri, New Mexico, Rhode Island, South Dakota, Washington.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Modified Comparative Negligence — 50% Bar</h3>
        <p>You can recover only if your fault is less than 50%. If you are exactly 50% at fault or more, you recover nothing. If you are 49% at fault on a $100,000 claim, you recover $51,000. If you are 51% at fault, you recover $0.</p>
        <p><strong>50% bar states include:</strong> Arkansas, Georgia, Idaho, Kansas, Maine, Nebraska, North Dakota, Oregon, Tennessee, Utah, West Virginia.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Modified Comparative Negligence — 51% Bar</h3>
        <p>The most common system. You can recover if your fault is 50% or less. If you are 51% or more at fault, you recover nothing.</p>
        <p><strong>51% bar states include:</strong> Colorado, Connecticut, Delaware, Hawaii, Illinois, Indiana, Iowa, Massachusetts, Michigan, Minnesota, Montana, Nevada, New Hampshire, New Jersey, Ohio, Oklahoma, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, Texas, Vermont, Wisconsin, Wyoming.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Contributory Negligence</h3>
        <p>The strictest and least common system. Under contributory negligence, <strong>any fault on your part — even 1% — completely bars your recovery.</strong> If you were even slightly negligent, you get nothing, even if the other driver was 99% responsible.</p>
        <p><strong>Contributory negligence states:</strong> Alabama, Maryland, North Carolina, Virginia, and Washington D.C.</p>
        <p>If you live in a contributory negligence state and the insurer is trying to assign you even minor fault, consult an attorney immediately.</p>

